1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Moving on to skeletal muscle, the first disorder that we're going to discuss is dermatomyositis.

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:06,460
Now the name here tells you a lot.

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,980
You've got myositis, which is inflammation of the muscle,

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:12,360
and dermato, which implies that you have inflammation of the skin.

5
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:17,320
And so this is going to be an inflammatory disorder of both the skin and the skeletal muscle.

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,840
The etiology is not known, but what is particularly high yield

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:25,640
is that some cases of dermatomyositis are associated with carcinoma,

8
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:27,400
for example, gastric carcinoma.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,160
Board examiners like you to know that if a patient presents with dermatomyositis,

10
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:36,480
you need to consider the possibility of an underlying carcinoma, especially in the stomach.

11
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:43,200
The clinical features of dermatomyositis are that you'll get bilateral proximal muscle weakness,

12
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,260
that's high yield, and by proximal muscle weakness,

13
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,640
the way by which the patients would present would classically be they will say that they can't comb their hair,

14
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:55,000
because when you raise your arm to comb your hair, you need to use the shoulders,

15
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:56,760
and they can't climb the stairs.

16
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,740
Now, late in the disease, they can develop distal involvement,

17
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:02,620
but again, the hallmark is proximal muscle weakness.

18
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:08,280
In addition, we would expect to see skin changes, and there are three key skin changes that you look for.

19
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:15,260
Number one is a rash of the upper eyelids, and this gives a little bit of a purple discoloration to the upper eyelids.

20
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:20,240
Number two, a malar rash, and I think that this is particularly high yield,

21
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,320
because board examiners like to go after this,

22
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:27,060
and the reason they like to go after it is because you classically associate a malar rash with lupus,

23
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,180
but you need to remember that you can also have a malar rash in dermatomyositis,

24
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:35,360
and that, again, is particularly high yield, because it's one of those knee jerks that, you know,

25
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,440
once you see the word malar rash, you think you're done, and you pick lupus.

26
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,340
However, actually, you were dealing with dermatomyositis.

27
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:45,860
And the third rash would be these red papules on the elbow, knuckles, and knees,

28
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,000
which are also known as groton lesions.

29
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,580
And again, so again, you're going to have muscle weakness plus some skin finding.

30
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:55,440
On laboratory exam, the patients will have an increased creatinine kinase.

31
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:59,440
Creatinine kinase is an enzyme present in muscle, so when it's damaged,

32
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,680
so when muscle is damaged, this will leak out into the blood.

33
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:06,760
You'll have a positive ANA with an anti-JO1 antibody.

34
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:09,020
A JO1 antibody is very important.

35
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:10,720
It's the hallmark of this disease.

36
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:12,980
Be careful about the positive ANA.

37
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:16,380
If they tell you on the board exam that there's a positive ANA and a malar rash,

38
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,460
don't go after lupus until you also have a confirmatory test,

39
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:21,480
like a double-stranded DNA antibody.

40
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:26,460
Or, if you have an anti-JO1 antibody, then think about dermatomyositis.

41
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,320
The hallmark and one of the defining features of this disease

42
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:33,980
is that you have perimesial inflammation with perifascicular atrophy.

43
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:37,160
Now, in order to understand that, let's go back and remind ourselves

44
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:38,880
this is the muscle fascicle.

45
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:43,580
And the fascicle is made up of a bunch of individual fibers.

46
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,460
Now, the connective tissue that sits out here at the edge of the fascicle,

47
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:50,340
this is called the perimesium.

48
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,780
And the connective tissue that sits in between the fibers,

49
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,840
this is called the endomysium.

50
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:01,940
Now, what happens in dermatomyositis is that you get inflammation at the perimesium,

51
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:09,060
so out at the edge of the muscle fascicle, which then results in perifascicular atrophy,

52
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:12,180
or atrophy of the muscle with inflammation out at the edge.

53
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:17,320
This is very high yield because it helps to distinguish dermatomyositis from another entity

54
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,740
that we're going to talk about, which is polymyositis,

55
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,780
because in polymyositis, you're going to have endomysial inflammation.

56
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:27,400
And the way I remember this, i.e., that is it endomysial or perimesial,

57
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:31,340
is that the perimesium is out at the edge, and so it's closer to the skin.

58
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:35,920
And so, in dermatomyositis, you're going to have perimesial inflammation,

59
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:38,220
which is closer to the skin, and so therefore,

60
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:42,180
it sort of matches with the concept of dermatomyositis.

61
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,900
Now, again, that's just one way to remember it, but I think it's helpful.

62
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:47,480
Here's an image to highlight the same principle.

63
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:50,100
This is a muscle fascicle,

64
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:55,040
and you'll notice that you've got inflammation out in the connective tissue

65
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:57,020
at the edge of the muscle fascicle,

66
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:00,340
resulting in some atrophy of the muscle cells.

67
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:02,780
Now, notice that this is the normal size of the muscle cell,

68
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:05,240
and can you notice that these here are smaller,

69
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:07,240
hence they've become atrophic.

70
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,500
And so, again, perimesial inflammation

71
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:13,240
with perifascicular atrophy of the muscle.

72
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,200
And so the way by which I remember this is,

73
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:18,300
let's assume the skin is way out here, way above in this section,

74
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:21,640
then this would be sort of closer to the skin,

75
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,660
and that's how I remember that it's part of dermatomyositis.

76
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:27,380
One final point concerning dermatomyositis,

77
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:29,540
and that is that the treatment is corticosteroids.

78
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,600
Of course, this is an inflammatory disorder,

79
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,380
and corticosteroids would limit the inflammation.

80
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:38,660
Polyomyositis is an inflammatory disorder of skeletal muscle.

81
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:41,220
It resembles dermatomyositis,

82
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:43,380
especially with the proximal muscle weakness.

83
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:45,500
However, the skin will not be involved.

84
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:47,740
So you will not see malar rash,

85
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,720
you will not see a purplish rash of the upper eyelids,

86
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:53,240
and you will not see Groton's lesions

87
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:55,580
on the knees and the elbows, for example.

88
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:59,300
In addition, one key distinction is that in polymyositis,

89
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:03,040
you get endomysial inflammation with necrotic muscle fibers.

90
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:05,900
Again, this endomysial is particularly high yield,

91
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:08,100
so here you've got the muscle fascicle

92
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:10,480
made up of a bunch of fibers, as I've already illustrated.

93
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:12,320
This was the perimesium.

94
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:14,180
This is the endomysium.

95
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:17,140
And so what's going to happen in polymyositis

96
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:19,220
is that you'll have inflammation scattered

97
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,740
throughout this muscle fascicle

98
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:23,640
between all of the fibers.

99
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:26,300
Again, you'll have endomysial inflammation.

100
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:29,260
Moving on to X-linked muscular dystrophy,

101
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,660
this is a degenerative disorder characterized by muscle wasting,

102
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:35,400
but the most important and most high yield point

103
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,480
concerning this disorder

104
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:39,200
is that you get replacement of skeletal muscle

105
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:40,300
by adipose tissue.

106
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:42,840
In fact, that's really what's meant by dystrophy.

107
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:44,900
And so X-linked muscular dystrophy,

108
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:47,340
replacement of skeletal muscle by adipose tissue.

109
00:05:47,940 --> 00:05:49,060
Now, it's high yield to know

110
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:51,440
that this is due to deletions of dystrophin,

111
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,680
and there's a few points that I'd like to make clear,

112
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:55,020
all of which show up on exams.

113
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:58,180
Number one, it is due to deletions of dystrophin.

114
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:00,780
This is also known as Duchenne's muscular dystrophy,

115
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:04,100
and remember that Duchenne's is also due to deletions,

116
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:06,120
and so they both begin with a D.

117
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:08,680
The second point that you have to know

118
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:11,600
is that mutations are often spontaneous in this gene.

119
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:13,100
Now, the reason this happens

120
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:15,940
is because this gene is the largest gene in the human genome,

121
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,020
and so therefore, because of its large size,

122
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:20,400
there's an increased risk for spontaneous mutations,

123
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:22,680
and the third point is that dystrophin

124
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:23,760
is an anchor molecule

125
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:25,700
that angles the muscle cytoskeleton

126
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:27,160
to the extracellular matrix.

127
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,340
Now, imagine if this is a muscle cell,

128
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:31,240
and you've got all of your filaments in it,

129
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:33,740
then dystrophin is sort of this linker molecule

130
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:36,060
that allows you to then interact

131
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:37,900
with the extracellular matrix.

132
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,860
It's one of many proteins that do so,

133
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:42,280
but it is an essential protein, again,

134
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:44,580
that links the muscle cytoskeleton

135
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:46,100
to the extracellular matrix.

136
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:49,300
Patients with Duchenne's X-linked muscular dystrophy,

137
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:52,380
they present with proximal muscle weakness

138
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:54,180
classically at one year of age,

139
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:56,400
so the disease begins proximally,

140
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,180
for example, in the thigh and the hip,

141
00:06:58,520 --> 00:06:59,800
and then eventually progresses

142
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,140
to involve the distal muscle.

143
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:03,320
Now, the hallmark of this sequence

144
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:04,940
is calf pseudohypertrophy.

145
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,480
Let me just explain this.

146
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:07,960
So at about one year of age,

147
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:09,520
the child is learning how to walk.

148
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:11,540
Now, when the child is learning how to walk,

149
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:12,820
at the same time,

150
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:15,560
the proximal muscles are going to start becoming weak.

151
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:17,440
When the proximal muscles become weak,

152
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:20,000
the child is then going to put more force

153
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,440
on the calf muscle in order to walk.

154
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,800
And as they put more force on the calf muscle,

155
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:27,020
the calf muscle is going to undergo hypertrophy.

156
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:29,260
Now, over time, the disease will progress

157
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:30,580
and then come distally.

158
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:32,020
And as it comes distally,

159
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:33,200
it's going to hit the calf,

160
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:35,420
and when it hits the hypertrophied calf,

161
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:37,040
it's going to convert the calf

162
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:38,740
from muscle into fat,

163
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,780
because that's the hallmark of muscular dystrophy.

164
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,580
And so then,

165
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:44,980
it'll look like the calf muscle is big.

166
00:07:45,260 --> 00:07:46,820
However, were you to put your hand on it,

167
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:48,300
it would actually be full of fat.

168
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:50,500
So that's why they call it pseudohypertrophy.

169
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:51,900
The calf does look big.

170
00:07:52,140 --> 00:07:55,080
However, it's just really full of fat instead of muscle.

171
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:56,220
In addition,

172
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:58,140
the creatinine kinase will be elevated.

173
00:07:58,540 --> 00:07:59,000
And again,

174
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:00,860
the reason that the creatinine kinase is elevated

175
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:02,820
is that you have damage to muscle cells,

176
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:04,640
and creatinine kinase is an enzyme

177
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:06,040
present within muscle cells.

178
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:08,780
Death in this disorder results from cardiac

179
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:10,200
or respiratory failure.

180
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,180
Eventually, the myocardium or the diaphragm

181
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:13,940
will become involved,

182
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:17,260
resulting in cardiac or respiratory failure.

183
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:17,920
And again,

184
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,200
the myocardium is commonly involved.

185
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:21,780
Becker muscular dystrophy

186
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:23,460
is the exact same disease

187
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:25,780
as Duchenne X-linked muscular dystrophy.

188
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:26,580
However,

189
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:27,980
remember that in Duchenne's,

190
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:29,600
you had a deletion of dystropin,

191
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:31,020
whereas in Becker's,

192
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:33,060
you have a mutation of dystropin.

193
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:35,460
Now this mutation allows for some of the protein

194
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:36,240
to be produced,

195
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:37,140
and so therefore,

196
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:39,340
you get clinically milder disease.

197
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:40,640
So it's very similar.

198
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:41,220
However,

199
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,060
the patients have clinically milder disease.

200
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:43,620
And again,

201
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:44,440
the hallmark would be

202
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:47,020
focal replacement of skeletal muscle with fat

203
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:48,820
and also muscular weakness.

204
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:50,860
Moving on to the neuromuscular junction,

205
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:52,540
there's two disorders we want to cover.

206
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:53,140
However,

207
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,000
before we do that,

208
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:55,120
we need a little bit of background.

209
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:56,600
So let's remind ourselves

210
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:58,780
that the neuromuscular junction has a nerve.

211
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,980
It contains acetylcholine,

212
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:04,400
and it has a calcium channel here

213
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:06,600
that, when open,

214
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:08,720
allows for the release of acetylcholine.

215
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:10,860
There is also a muscle plate

216
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:12,960
with acetylcholine receptors,

217
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:14,540
and it's these receptors

218
00:09:14,540 --> 00:09:15,840
that bind acetylcholine

219
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:17,860
and then cause firing of the muscle.

220
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:20,420
Now the first disorder is myasthenia gravis,

221
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:21,220
and in this disorder,

222
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:22,360
you have an antibody

223
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:24,300
against these receptors.

224
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:26,940
The antibody does not destroy the receptors.

225
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:28,840
It merely competes with acetylcholine

226
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:30,140
at the level of the receptor.

227
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:31,640
So what's going to happen

228
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:33,920
is the first time you have firing of the nerve

229
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:35,120
and you release acetylcholine,

230
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,180
let's imagine that 50 molecules

231
00:09:37,180 --> 00:09:38,460
of acetylcholine are released.

232
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:40,120
They'll compete with the antibody,

233
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:41,320
kick off the antibody,

234
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,560
and allow for a proper contraction of the muscle.

235
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:44,460
However,

236
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:46,540
with rapid contraction of the muscle,

237
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:48,240
you'll have rapid firing of the nerve,

238
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:49,900
and you'll only be able to release,

239
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:50,480
for example,

240
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:51,980
the next time 40 molecules,

241
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:52,520
and then,

242
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:53,180
for example,

243
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:54,660
the next time 30 molecules

244
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:55,500
of acetylcholine,

245
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:56,260
et cetera,

246
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:57,260
until you get to the point

247
00:09:57,260 --> 00:09:58,900
where you're releasing fewer molecules

248
00:09:58,900 --> 00:09:59,620
of acetylcholine

249
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:00,540
with each contraction.

250
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:01,520
Now,

251
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:01,940
once,

252
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:03,120
now as that occurs,

253
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:04,200
and for example,

254
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:05,420
you're only releasing 10,

255
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:06,600
that's going to decrease

256
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:07,920
the ability of acetylcholine

257
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,220
to compete with the antibody,

258
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:10,640
and so over time,

259
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:12,000
with repeated contractions,

260
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:13,680
you actually get muscle weakness.

261
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:15,080
Then if there's rest,

262
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:16,800
you build up additional acetylcholine,

263
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:17,360
again,

264
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:17,820
then you,

265
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:19,000
you contract the muscle,

266
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:20,600
and then you release 50 again,

267
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:22,140
which can easily compete

268
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:23,120
with this,

269
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:24,500
which can easily compete

270
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:25,780
with these antibodies.

271
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:27,600
So this is the hallmark

272
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:29,100
of myasthenia gravis.

273
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:29,720
Now,

274
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:30,800
another important point here

275
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:31,940
is that when you give the patient

276
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:33,600
anticholinaristerase agents,

277
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:34,580
the symptoms improve.

278
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:35,760
The idea is

279
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:37,520
that the anticholinaristerase agents

280
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:38,900
allow the acetylcholine

281
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,600
that's coming from the nerve

282
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:42,040
to last for a longer time,

283
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:43,720
and if it lasts for a longer time,

284
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:45,240
it'll better be able to compete

285
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:46,620
against these antibodies,

286
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:47,380
and therefore,

287
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:48,600
it'll improve the symptoms.

288
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:50,460
So with that background in mind,

289
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:52,160
let's move on to myasthenia gravis.

290
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:54,020
So as your text states,

291
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:55,720
this is an autoantibody

292
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:57,140
against the postsynaptic

293
00:10:57,140 --> 00:10:58,300
acetylcholine receptor

294
00:10:58,300 --> 00:10:59,780
at the neuromuscular junction,

295
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:01,580
more commonly seen in women.

296
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:02,120
Of course,

297
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:03,600
it's an autoimmune disease.

298
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:04,620
Clinically,

299
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,240
the features will be muscle weakness

300
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,740
that worsens with use.

301
00:11:07,860 --> 00:11:08,080
Again,

302
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:09,440
you're releasing less and less

303
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:10,180
acetylcholine

304
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,240
as you rapidly use the muscle,

305
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:14,640
and it improves with rest,

306
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:15,320
and that's because

307
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:17,000
if you give the neuromuscular junction

308
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:17,820
time to rest,

309
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:19,240
additional acetylcholine

310
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:19,900
will build up

311
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:20,780
within that nerve.

312
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:22,000
Classically,

313
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,080
it involves the eyes,

314
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:23,920
leading to ptosis

315
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:24,760
and double vision,

316
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:26,660
and that's an important hallmark

317
00:11:26,660 --> 00:11:27,260
that would show up

318
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:28,240
in a clinical vignette.

319
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:30,160
The symptoms will improve

320
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:32,280
with anticholonesterase agents.

321
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:32,820
Again,

322
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:33,960
the longer acetylcholine

323
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:34,480
is present

324
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:35,680
in the neuromuscular junction,

325
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:37,140
the better it will compete

326
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:38,100
with those antibodies,

327
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:40,020
and very high yield,

328
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:41,060
it's associated

329
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:42,180
with thymic hyperplasia

330
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:42,980
or thymoma.

331
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:43,600
This shows up

332
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:44,820
on the board exams often,

333
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:46,520
and this is important

334
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:48,160
because if there's thymic hyperplasia

335
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:49,020
or thymoma,

336
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:51,240
and you remove the thymus,

337
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:52,320
the patient's symptoms

338
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:53,060
will improve.

339
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:53,900
And so again,

340
00:11:53,980 --> 00:11:55,480
this is particularly high yield

341
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:56,280
that the patients

342
00:11:56,280 --> 00:11:57,620
can have thymic hyperplasia

343
00:11:57,620 --> 00:11:58,600
or thymoma.

344
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:00,080
Eaton-Lambert syndrome

345
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:01,200
is when you have an antibody

346
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:03,480
against the presynaptic calcium channels

347
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:04,780
at the neuromuscular junction.

348
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:06,400
Now recall that

349
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:07,340
if this was the nerve

350
00:12:07,340 --> 00:12:08,660
and this was the plate here,

351
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:10,640
there was a calcium channel here

352
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:12,480
that when open

353
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:14,240
resulted in the release

354
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:15,120
of acetylcholine.

355
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:16,040
So imagine

356
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:17,300
if you have an antibody

357
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:18,880
against the calcium channel,

358
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:20,160
you're not going to be able

359
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:21,300
to release acetylcholine,

360
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:22,340
and so you're going to get

361
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:23,040
muscle weakness.

362
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:24,840
Now this is classically seen

363
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:26,500
as a perineoplastic syndrome,

364
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:27,580
most commonly due

365
00:12:27,580 --> 00:12:28,780
to small cell carcinoma.

366
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:30,260
Again, you'll get impaired

367
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:31,460
release of acetylcholine,

368
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:32,640
and that'll then result

369
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:33,500
in muscle weakness.

370
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:34,760
Now very high yield

371
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:35,960
to note in this disorder

372
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:37,440
that the proximal muscles

373
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:38,080
are involved

374
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:38,940
and the eyes

375
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:40,140
are usually spared.

376
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:41,080
In addition,

377
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:42,560
the anticholonesterase agents

378
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:43,780
will not improve the symptoms.

379
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:44,700
And of course,

380
00:12:44,820 --> 00:12:45,760
because if you're not able

381
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:47,420
to release acetylcholine,

382
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:48,800
then you're not going

383
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:49,180
to be able

384
00:12:49,180 --> 00:12:50,320
to improve the symptoms

385
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:53,080
with anticholonesterase agents.

386
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:54,380
And so we say

387
00:12:54,380 --> 00:12:55,640
that in Lambert-Eaton syndrome,

388
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:56,660
you have antibodies

389
00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:58,260
against the presynaptic

390
00:12:58,260 --> 00:12:59,360
calcium channels

391
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:00,660
at the neuromuscular junction.

392
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:02,920
It's a perineoplastic syndrome

393
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,620
related to small cell carcinoma,

394
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:05,880
and it impairs

395
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:07,540
release of acetylcholine.

396
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:09,140
The proximal muscles

397
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:09,800
will be weak,

398
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:10,720
and the weakness

399
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:12,060
will improve with use.

400
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:13,640
Let me just quickly explain this.

401
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:14,300
The reason why

402
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:15,160
the weakness improves

403
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:15,560
with use

404
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:16,340
is that you've got

405
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:17,360
an antibody here

406
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,460
competing with the calcium channel.

407
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:20,760
And so what's going to happen

408
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,240
is the more you fire this nerve,

409
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:23,080
the more there's going

410
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:23,940
to be a gradient

411
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:25,360
trying to force calcium in.

412
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:26,120
And so again,

413
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:27,260
the more you use the nerve,

414
00:13:27,360 --> 00:13:28,100
the more the pressure

415
00:13:28,100 --> 00:13:29,140
to force calcium in,

416
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:30,040
and you'll be able

417
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:30,780
to then kick off

418
00:13:30,780 --> 00:13:31,520
this receptor.

419
00:13:31,940 --> 00:13:33,120
And so the muscle weakness

420
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:34,260
will actually improve

421
00:13:34,260 --> 00:13:34,780
with use.

422
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:37,140
And the eyes are usually spared,

423
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:38,320
particularly high yield.

424
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:38,940
Again,

425
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:40,380
anticholonesterase agents

426
00:13:40,380 --> 00:13:41,700
don't improve the symptoms,

427
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:43,400
and if you remove the tumor,

428
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:45,220
the perineoplastic syndrome

429
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:45,860
will disappear.

430
00:13:46,580 --> 00:13:47,500
The final section

431
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:48,080
in this chapter

432
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:49,160
is soft tissue tumors.

433
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:50,000
This is going to be

434
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,120
a very brief overview

435
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:52,920
of just a couple tumors.

436
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:54,640
The first is lipoma.

437
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:55,620
Lipo means fat,

438
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:56,820
and oma means mass,

439
00:13:56,880 --> 00:13:58,300
so this is a benign tumor

440
00:13:58,300 --> 00:13:59,200
of adipose tissue.

441
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:00,500
Important to know,

442
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:01,380
it's the most common

443
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:03,040
benign soft tissue tumor

444
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:03,880
in adults.

445
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:06,000
Liposarcoma.

446
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:07,000
Lipo means fat,

447
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:07,960
and sarcoma means

448
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:09,540
malignant mesenchymal tumor.

449
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:11,440
So this is a malignant tumor

450
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:12,280
of adipose tissue.

451
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:13,500
Important to know,

452
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:14,440
it's the most common

453
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,000
malignant soft tissue tumor

454
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:16,740
in adults.

455
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:17,500
Also,

456
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:18,220
the board examiners

457
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:18,860
like you to know

458
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:20,480
that the lipoblast

459
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:21,760
is the characteristic cell.

460
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:22,680
So if they tell you

461
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:23,440
that there's a mass

462
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:24,640
and the pathology report

463
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:25,840
says that lipoblasts

464
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:26,280
are present,

465
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:27,060
you're thinking

466
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:28,440
about liposarcoma.

467
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:30,660
Rhabdomyosarcoma.

468
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:32,680
Rhabdomyo means skeletal muscle,

469
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:34,280
and oma refers to mass,

470
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:36,120
and so this is a benign tumor

471
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:37,100
of skeletal muscle.

472
00:14:37,620 --> 00:14:37,760
Now,

473
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:38,540
you can also have

474
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:40,140
a cardiac rhabdomyoma

475
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:41,460
and very high yield

476
00:14:41,460 --> 00:14:42,020
to recall,

477
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:43,020
and we talked about this

478
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:43,960
in the cardiac lectures,

479
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:45,380
that this is associated

480
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:47,080
with tuberous sclerosis.

481
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:48,920
Rhabdomyosarcoma,

482
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:49,240
again,

483
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:50,120
rhabdomyo,

484
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:51,460
skeletal muscle,

485
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:52,840
and sarcoma,

486
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:54,320
malignant mesenchymal tumor.

487
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:56,920
So this is a malignant tumor

488
00:14:56,920 --> 00:14:57,760
of skeletal muscle.

489
00:14:57,760 --> 00:14:58,860
Important to know,

490
00:14:58,960 --> 00:14:59,800
it's the most common

491
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:01,440
malignant soft tissue tumor

492
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:01,980
in children.

493
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:03,360
Most common malignant

494
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:04,440
soft tissue tumor

495
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:04,920
in children.

496
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:06,660
The characteristic cell

497
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:07,760
is called a rhabdomyoblast.

498
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:09,960
Board examiners like you

499
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:10,880
to know that this cell

500
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:12,180
is Desmond positive.

501
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:14,300
The most common sight

502
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:15,440
is the head and neck.

503
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:16,260
However,

504
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:18,240
in a young girl,

505
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:19,640
especially around

506
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:20,580
the age of five,

507
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:21,560
the vagina

508
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:22,780
is a classic sight.

509
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:23,320
And remember,

510
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:24,020
we talked about

511
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:24,560
a disorder

512
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:25,560
in the gynae lectures

513
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:26,920
called sarcoma botrytes,

514
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:27,900
which I told you

515
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:28,440
at that point

516
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:29,720
was also an embryonal

517
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:30,940
rhabdomyosarcoma.

518
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:32,240
This was the young girl

519
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:33,480
with a grape-like mass

520
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:34,660
protruding from the vagina.

521
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:35,700
And I told you

522
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:36,420
that characteristic

523
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:37,340
on biopsy,

524
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:39,120
you would see rhabdomyoblast

525
00:15:39,120 --> 00:15:40,420
with striations

526
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:41,320
within the cells

527
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:42,200
and then cells

528
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:42,560
that were

529
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:43,500
Desmond positive

530
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:44,040
as well.

531
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:44,980
And so this

532
00:15:44,980 --> 00:15:46,040
is what I'm referring to

533
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:46,940
when I say that

534
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:47,860
the vagina

535
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:48,960
is a classic sight

536
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:49,600
in young girls.

537
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:50,240
And that's probably

538
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:51,260
the highest yield

539
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:52,700
rhabdomyosarcoma

540
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:53,500
that would show up

541
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:54,320
on a board exam.

542
00:15:54,820 --> 00:15:55,780
And that concludes

543
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:56,580
both the section

544
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:57,300
and the chapter.

